<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <AssemblyName>StateBridge</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>StateBridge</RootNamespace>
    <EnableHarmony>false</EnableHarmony>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Pathoschild.Stardew.ModBuildConfig" Version="4.3.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    "Every action drives the game; it does not write the outcome" is a RULE, and a rule that a person
    checks by hand is a suggestion. This one is checked by the compiler: a banned primitive cannot be
    built into the bridge at all - not by accident, not inside a helper, not behind a fallback.

    That was not hypothetical. `Game1.warpFarmer` sat inside EnterBed while `warp` was already declared
    illegal in the action table, and planting charged nothing for a whole field of parsnips. Both were
    found by widening a scan, which only happened because a person went looking. This target means
    nobody has to go looking again.

    The patterns live in agent/authenticity.py, so what the build forbids and what the runtime audit
    reports can never drift apart.
  -->
  <Target Name="FidelityCheck" BeforeTargets="Build">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\..\.venv\Scripts\python.exe&quot; &quot;$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\..\tools\check_fidelity.py&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
